hey Pat what's up Ryoga I mean crisis  mode right now my boss is asking a minor  I don't know what to do okay slow down  and don't worry in the next few minutes  we will lay out the five easy steps to  kick-start your Google cloud migration  journey wow that would be awesome thanks  so much  okay Pat hang on the line and you out  stay tuned  [Music]  hi everyone I'm Priyanka and welcome to  cloud migration 101 where we will talk  about all things migration in this first  episode I'll show you how to get started  with your migration journey to Google  cloud what is called migration anyway it  typically refers to moving apps deployed  on-premise ease into the cloud mainly to  leverage the benefits of cloud like  reduced cost increased security  flexibility and speed it could also mean  moving workloads from one cloud to  another any type of moving can be a  challenge putting things in write boxes  what if something gets lost or breaks  can you put it all back together well  cloud migration is no different even for  the most experienced teams so you need  to plan and execute your migration very  carefully where do we get started  well the first step in the migration  journey to Google cloud town is to  define your starting point now starting  point could be on-premises where you  manage the entire infrastructure and the  apps it could be in private hosting  environment where you manage the  virtualization and resources and the  rest is managed by the service provider  or it could be in another cloud now that  we have defined the starting and the end  points we are ready to jump into stop  number 2 the types of workloads to  migrate this is important because it  helps us assess the complexity of the  migration there can be different kinds  of workloads but for our purposes today  it is safe to categorize them as legacy  workloads and cloud native workloads  legacy workloads are developed without  any consideration for cloud environment  so it can be difficult and frankly  expensive to modify them to run on cloud  on the other hand cloud native workloads  are natively scalable portable available  and secure so consider the type of  workload you're dealing with while  planning a migration now we are at stop  3 it's time to pick the type of  migration that makes sense  there's lift and shift where you move  workloads from a source to a target  environment with minor or no  modifications this is ideal when a  workload can operate as is in the target  environment or when there's little or no  business need for change it also  requires the least amount of time and  are easiest to accomplish  the disadvantages you have a non cloud  native workload running in Google cloud  now which is not ideal to take full  advantage of cloud platform features  like horizontal scaling pricing and  managed services the second type of  migration is improve and move in which  you modernize the workload while  migrating it to take advantage of cloud  native capabilities this may take longer  than the lift and shift because you're  refactoring but it turns out to be  better in the long run because you gain  access to many of the cloud native  benefits the third type is rip and  replace migration where you decommission  an existing app and completely redesign  and rewrite it as a cloud native app you  would do this if your current app is too  expensive to migrate or you don't want  to maintain it this will obviously take  a lot longer but the upside is this  migration sets you up to take full  advantage of those amazing cloud  features including the horizontal  scaling managed services and high  availability  once you decide which migration type  makes sense for your business workload  we move on to the fourth stop on our  migration journey assess your readiness  for cloud your readiness depends on four  themes learning programs leadership  support experience to scale cloud native  services and the capacity to protect  your current environment for each theme  you should be in one of the three phases  tactical strategic or transformational  since this is an exercise that highly  depends on your organization's maturity  for adopting cloud technologies click  here to learn how to evaluate your  organization on the cloud maturity scale  stock 5 on our journey is to define the  core of the migration the migration path  it has four phases you assess you plan  you deploy and then you optimize got it  now let me let me try that again a  little bit slower in the assess phase  you perform a thorough assessment and  discovery of your existing environment  to understand your app inventory  identify any dependencies and  requirements perform total cost of  ownership calculations and establish app  performance benchmarks in the plan phase  you create the basic cloud  infrastructure for your apps and plan  how you will move them this planning  includes creating an organization  folders environments projects resources  and networks along with access controls  sorting the apps and developing a  prioritized migration strategy in deploy  phase you design implement and execute  deployment processes to move workloads  to Google Cloud and in the optimized  space you begin to take full advantage  of cloud native technologies such as  performance scalability disaster  recovery cost training as well as  opening the doors to machine learning  and artificial intelligence integrations  for your apps here is the summary of  five steps we defined to kickstart your  migration journey to Google Cloud first  you define the starting point and end  point which is Google Cloud then you  define your workloads followed by the  migration types then using the cloud  adoption framework assess your readiness  for your cloud journey and finally get  set for the migration path assess plant  deploy and optimize now that we have a  starting point  Pat is off building their migration plan  to Google Cloud you all stay tuned for  the next episode where we will help you  choose what to migrate first if you've  got thoughts on different methods you've  used leave a comment below so we can  continue our conversation until then  like subscribe to keep learning about  cloud migration  oh and check out the more detailed  getting started guide by our Solutions  Architect Marko fire
 

